\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise*

\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise*
Allow 50 minutes. There are 50 practice points. In each market \begin_inset Formula $t$\end_inset
, a unit mass of consumers chooses at most one of two inside products \begin_inset Formula $j=1,2$\end_inset
, or the outside option \begin_inset Formula $j=0$\end_inset
. Product \begin_inset Formula $j$\end_inset
 has an observed scalar characteristic \begin_inset Formula $x_{jt}$\end_inset
, coefficient \begin_inset Formula $\beta$\end_inset
, and quality \begin_inset Formula $\xi_{jt}$\end_inset
 that consumers and firms know but an analyst may not observe. Write \begin_inset Formula $\delta_{jt}=\beta x_{jt}+\xi_{jt}$\end_inset
. Consumer \begin_inset Formula $i$\end_inset
 obtains \begin_inset Formula $u_{ijt}=\delta_{jt}-\alpha p_{jt}+\varepsilon_{ijt}$\end_inset
 from an inside product and \begin_inset Formula $u_{i0t}=\varepsilon_{i0t}$\end_inset
 from the outside option. Here \begin_inset Formula $p_{jt}\geq0$\end_inset
 is a price, \begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$\end_inset
 is the marginal utility cost of a unit of expenditure, and all \begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{ijt}$\end_inset
 are independent across consumers, products and markets with distribution function \begin_inset Formula $F(e)=\exp\{-\exp(-e)\}$\end_inset
. The outside option has price and deterministic utility zero. Consumers take prices as given; income constraints do not bind in this quasilinear demand specification.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise*
You may use the logit formula: if \begin_inset Formula $v_{jt}:=\delta_{jt}-\alpha p_{jt}$\end_inset
, then \begin_inset Formula $s_{jt}=e^{v_{jt}}/(1+e^{v_{1t}}+e^{v_{2t}})$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $s_{0t}=1/(1+e^{v_{1t}}+e^{v_{2t}})$\end_inset
. These shares are probabilities over the whole market, including consumers who buy neither inside product.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise*
(a) Derive \begin_inset Formula $\log(s_{jt}/s_{0t})$\end_inset
 and all own and cross price derivatives of the shares, holding qualities fixed. For \begin_inset Formula $k\ne j$\end_inset
, including \begin_inset Formula $k=0$\end_inset
, define the diversion ratio from \begin_inset Formula $j$\end_inset
 to \begin_inset Formula $k$\end_inset
 as \begin_inset Formula $D_{j\to k}:=(\partial s_{kt}/\partial p_{jt})/(-\partial s_{jt}/\partial p_{jt})$\end_inset
. Derive it and explain what the formula does, and does not, say about products that are near one another in characteristic space. [12 points]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise*
(b) Each product is sold by a different firm. Firm \begin_inset Formula $j$\end_inset
 has constant marginal cost \begin_inset Formula $c_j>0$\end_inset
, no fixed cost or capacity constraint, and chooses \begin_inset Formula $p_j\geq0$\end_inset
 to maximize \begin_inset Formula $\pi_j=(p_j-c_j)s_j$\end_inset
. It observes qualities and takes the rival price as given. Derive its interior pricing condition and express the markup \begin_inset Formula $p_j-c_j$\end_inset
 using \begin_inset Formula $\alpha$\end_inset
 and its share. Then prove that the condition identifies the unique global best response, not merely a stationary price. You may first exclude prices below cost and then study how the sign of the profit derivative changes. Do not assume that the profit function is globally concave. [14 points]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise*
(c) Set \begin_inset Formula $\alpha=1$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $\beta x_{jt}=0$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $c_1=c_2=1/2$\end_inset
 in both markets. In market 1, both qualities are \begin_inset Formula $\xi_{j1}=2$\end_inset
 and a proposed price profile is \begin_inset Formula $p_{11}=p_{21}=2$\end_inset
. In market 2, both qualities are \begin_inset Formula $\xi_{j2}=9/4+\log3$\end_inset
 and a proposed profile is \begin_inset Formula $p_{12}=p_{22}=9/4$\end_inset
. Compute all three shares, each firm's markup and profit in each market. Verify that both profiles are Nash equilibria using the global best response argument. Within each market, hold its supplied qualities fixed when considering a price deviation. [12 points]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise*
(d) An analyst pools the four product observations and runs an ordinary least squares regression with an intercept of \begin_inset Formula $y_{jt}:=\log(s_{jt}/s_{0t})$\end_inset
 on \begin_inset Formula $p_{jt}$\end_inset
, omitting quality. Calculate the fitted price slope exactly. Compare it with the structural price coefficient in utility. Explain how a higher price and a higher share can occur together across these markets without violating downward sloping demand. State an economically plausible excluded cost shifter, the exclusion and relevance conditions it would need, and why two constructed markets do not establish those conditions. [12 points]
\end_layout

